        'en' => [
            'title' => 'Why the dialysis chair stays empty: worry alone does not book a replacement session',
            'image_alt' => 'In a deep violet room, an empty teal dialysis chair stands next to a monitor on the left, while on a desk to the right a lifted telephone receiver sends a glowing teal ribbon of light toward the chair, clean illustration without text.',
            'excerpt' => 'Nearly two-thirds of unplanned missed haemodialysis sessions outside hospital arise on the day of treatment, most often because of acute physical symptoms. Although half of patients worry about harming their health, only a quarter of them book a replacement slot on their own. What makes the difference is not another warning, but a phone call from the unit.',
            'content' => <<<'HTML'
<p>An empty dialysis chair invites a quick line in the chart: patient did not attend. In everyday routine, that note easily hides the assumption that the person underestimated treatment or chose other plans instead. Yet roughly half of all missed haemodialysis sessions happen because the patient is in hospital, and even the remaining absences outside hospital are not driven by people who chronically refuse care. In a survey by the research team of the NephroVira dialysis network, presented at an autumn nephrology congress in 2026 by Dr Elena Moretti, 76.2% of respondents had missed only a single session in the preceding thirty days.</p>
<p>A ten-question postal survey was sent to 3,998 adults aged 18 to 75 who missed at least one in-centre haemodialysis session for reasons other than hospital admission between 15 December 2025 and 6 January 2026. Between 26 January and 10 April 2026, 244 completed questionnaires were returned, although the accompanying interview with the author mentions 224 respondents and 235 people answered the question on the number of missed sessions in the prior month. A response rate of around 6.1% means the figures capture only the subset of patients who had the strength and willingness to return a paper form. Even in that group, however, a pattern emerged that clinicians see up close every week.</p>
<p>Fully 63.0% of missed sessions were unplanned: 46.7% of patients knew they would not attend only on the day of dialysis, and another 16.3% reported they had not known ahead of time at all. Only 15.0% knew one day before and 12.3% knew two to three days in advance. The main barrier was neither transport at 11.4% nor family or work obligations at 8.7%, but acute health problems, which accounted for 48.0% of cases; the remaining 32.0% fell under other reasons. A fifty-year-old patient summed it up in one sentence on the form: he did not feel well enough to come in. That is the everyday paradox of dialysis care: a person skips the very treatment meant to relieve their symptoms because they feel too unwell that morning to make the trip.</p>
<p>Patients know what a missed session can do. Nearly half of respondents, 49.1%, worried that missing dialysis would harm their health, while 46.1% were not worried and 4.7% preferred not to answer. They feared immediate physical symptoms they knew from experience far more than abstract clinical endpoints: 60.0% feared swelling, 55.0% tiredness and weakness, 41.0% shortness of breath, 37.0% hospitalisation and 34.0% heart problems. Worry alone, however, put nobody back in the chair. Among patients worried about health consequences, only 26.0% rescheduled their session, whereas 46.0% did not reschedule at all. Across the entire cohort, only 31.4% intended to book a replacement session, 4.0% planned merely to stay longer at the next treatment, 58.3% did not plan to make up the missed dialysis and 6.3% did not answer.</p>
<p>This gap explains why general lectures on the dangers of skipping dialysis fall short. Patients usually know why missing treatment is dangerous; when they stay home in the morning because of weakness, nausea or breathlessness, they do not need a reminder about long-term mortality, but a clear and simple way to arrange a replacement slot. In acute exhaustion, people put off complex arrangements even when they know missing treatment will hurt them. Yet when the dialysis unit called the patient proactively after a missed slot, 54% completed a rescheduled session, compared with 38% when no such outreach took place.</p>
<p>One timely phone call from the clinic joins two clinical tasks that an electronic absence code never solves. First, it serves as immediate clinical triage: a nurse or physician learns whether the patient is dealing with a passing upset at home or developing worsening breathlessness, fluid overload or warning signs of hyperkalaemia that require urgent evaluation. Second, it lowers the threshold for returning: instead of reproach, it offers a concrete open slot on the schedule and helps sort out transport. I discuss in greater detail why labelling a patient non-adherent obscures real barriers to care in the specialist article <a href="https://nefro.polascin.net/article.php?slug=skor-nez-pacienta-oznacime-nespolupracujuceho">Before we label a patient non-adherent</a> on Nefro-projekt.</p>
<p>When a dialysis chair stays empty, an administrative note closes the shift, but it does not close the patient's risk. The difference between a missed treatment and a safe return often lies not in a new protocol, but in whether someone at the unit picks up the phone and asks what happened this morning.</p>
<p><em>This article is for information only and does not replace individual medical advice. If you are on dialysis and develop sudden symptoms, shortness of breath, marked weakness or swelling, contact your dialysis unit or emergency medical services without delay. The name of the dialysis network, the researcher's name and other identifying details of third parties have been replaced with fictional details in this text; the numerical findings come from a survey presented in autumn 2026.</em></p>

